.TH SLURM-TOP 1 "2025" "slurm-top" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
slurm-top \- terminal dashboard for Slurm allocations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B slurm-top
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B slurm-top
queries Slurm node capacity with
.B scontrol show node -o
and queue information with
.BR squeue --json .
It displays scheduler allocations and pending requests; it does not report
sampled CPU, GPU, or memory utilization.
.PP
When the default
.B top
format writes to a terminal, it opens an alternate-screen interactive UI.
When output is not a terminal, it writes a one-time, pipe-friendly snapshot.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR --all
Include non-GPU nodes in node-oriented views.
.TP
.BR --format " " \fItop\fR|\fIjson\fR|\fIgrid\fR|\fIdetail\fR|\fItable
Select an output format. The default is
.BR top .
The compatibility value
.B dashboard
is an alias for
.BR grid .
.TP
.BR --sort " " \fIgpu\fR|\fIcpu\fR|\fImem\fR|\fIjobs\fR|\fIuser
Select the initial summary sort. The default is descending running GPU
allocation.
.TP
.BR --interactive
Require the alternate-screen interactive UI. It requires terminal standard
input and output.
.TP
.BR --watch " " \fIduration
Refresh at the supplied Go duration, such as
.BR 5s .
A zero duration prints once outside the interactive UI.
.TP
.BR --timeout " " \fIduration
Set the timeout for each Slurm query. The default is
.BR 10s .
.TP
.BR --prometheus-url " " \fIurl
Prometheus server URL for hardware utilization telemetry.
.TP
.BR --prometheus-user " " \fIuser
Prometheus HTTP basic auth username.
.TP
.BR --prometheus-password " " \fIpass
Prometheus HTTP basic auth password.
.TP
.B --no-prometheus
Disable Prometheus hardware utilization queries.
.TP
.BR -h , " " --help
Show command help.
.SH INTERACTIVE CONTROLS
The summary initially sorts running GPUs descending. Use Up and Down, or the
mouse wheel, to select a user. Right or Enter opens that user's job table.
Left or Escape returns to the summary and preserves its prior sort and selected user.
The menu shows USER, ACCOUNT, QOS, NODES, and EFFICIENCY; the active lower-pane view is highlighted. The
selected user's job table uses the USER menu label, and an account's job table uses the ACCOUNT menu label. Tab and Shift-Tab cycle
through USER, ACCOUNT, QOS, NODES, and EFFICIENCY. The selected user's or account's jobs open with Right or
Enter. USER, ACCOUNT, QOS, NODES, and EFFICIENCY can also be selected by clicking their menu items. A
horizontal rule separates the menu from the
current view. In QOS, a table displays Quality of Service allocations and pending
demand across sortable columns. Selecting a QoS row and pressing Right or Enter
opens that QoS's scrollable details view displaying job states, running allocations,
pending requests, limits, and runtime metrics; Escape or Left returns to the QOS table.
In NODES, directional arrows select a node in the displayed grid;
clicking a node or Enter replaces the grid with that node's scrollable details.
The details show node state and boot time followed by ALLOCATED and UTILIZED
boxes with GPU, GPU MEM, CPU, and memory bars. Utilization denominators match
the allocations above. When Prometheus telemetry is enabled, the UTILIZED box reports
active GPU count, total GPU VRAM usage from NVIDIA DCGM, active CPU core count,
and used host memory. Each running job on the node follows in a box labeled with
its ID, user, and account, showing observed compute duty cycle across allocated GPUs,
allocated GPU VRAM consumption, cgroup active CPU core usage, and cgroup host memory usage.
Node-detail telemetry is the mean of the 5 most recent readings taken 30 s apart, not a
job-lifetime figure; if a refresh fails, the last reading for that node is kept.
If Prometheus is disabled with --no-prometheus or unconfigured, unmeasured numerators
display as ? placeholders. Jobs with 0 GPUs allocated render 0% empty bars and 0/0 / 0/0G
without ? placeholders. Status bars in each column have uniform length across all groups.
H200 and H200-MIG allocations remain separate; generic GPUs remain generically labeled.
Escape returns to the grid, and the mouse wheel scrolls the details.
In EFFICIENCY, a table ranks all currently running jobs across the cluster by overall
resource efficiency score. Telemetry is automatically queried in the background on startup
and recalculated serially at most once a minute, so lifetime queries do not starve
node-detail lookups, maintaining a hot in-memory cache while keeping navigation instantaneous.
The score weights GPU compute duty cycle,
GPU VRAM usage, CPU core usage, and host memory usage equally across requested resource types,
normalized against Slurm requested/allocated resources. Each resource utilization is the median over the job's lifetime, computed by Prometheus
(quantile_over_time) from the job's start time. Samples are first combined at each instant:
job-level cgroup series (excluding per-step and per-task series) are summed across the job's
nodes, and DCGM series are summed across exactly the GPUs Slurm assigned on each node. GPU
VRAM usage is measured against the framebuffer of those GPUs. If a Prometheus query fails or
exceeds the 30-second calculation budget, the previous cached table is kept. When a job does not request GPUs (0 GPUs allocated), GPU compute and GPU memory
are weighted as 0 so they do not count towards the efficiency score calculation (which
evaluates requested CPU and host memory equally). The table defaults to sorting by least
efficient first (ascending score), with shortcuts s or e to sort by score.
Columns include JOB ID, USER, ACCOUNT, SCORE, ELAPSED, GPU, CPU, C:G, MEM, M:C, QOS,
PARTITION, and NAME.
.PP
Press Up from the first row to focus column headers. In header focus, use Left
and Right to choose a column and Enter to sort it descending; a subsequent
Enter changes the same column to ascending (or ascending first for SCORE). Clicking a column header also
sorts it. Clicking a summary row opens that user's jobs. The keys
.BR s ,
.BR e ,
.BR g ,
.BR c ,
.BR m ,
.BR j ,
.BR u ,
and
.B a
select efficiency score, GPU, CPU, memory, running-job, user, and account sorts.
.B r
refreshes and
.B q
or Ctrl-C exits.
.PP
A job table defaults to elapsed time descending. If it exceeds terminal width,
use Left and Right while a row is focused to scroll horizontally. Left at the
left edge returns to the summary.
.SH DISPLAY SEMANTICS
Running allocations use the Slurm
.B tres_alloc_str
field. Pending demand uses
.B tres_req_str
and is shown separately. Capacity is derived from
.BR scontrol .
CPU-per-GPU and memory-per-CPU values are ratios of scheduler allocations.
Selected GPU-node details show the node-reported operating-system boot time when
Slurm provides BootTime. Their utilization denominators are Slurm allocations,
and memory utilization is RealMemory minus FreeMem when both values are
comparable; CPU and GPU utilization are otherwise unknown. Memory table values
are decimal GB (displayed as G); aggregate memory summaries are decimal TB (displayed as T).
.PP
Job details include QoS, partition, progress, and elapsed/requested time.
Elapsed time is formatted as
.BR percent% " [" elapsed "|" requested "]" .
It is a schedule-time estimate, not measured completion. H200 and H200-MIG
bars use typed GPU TRES; generic or other GPU types are not included in those
typed bars.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set, disables supported terminal colors.
.TP
.B CLICOLOR
Set to
.B 0
to disable supported terminal colors.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/slurm-top
System package installation path.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/slurm-top/README.md
Package documentation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR squeue (1),
.BR scontrol (1)
